{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790439528,"nonce":"77+ecutzwqqByGW63FGtWWcfkRs=","title":"`#export = \"add(n: Num) -\u003e Num\"` on a method names what the member exposes to other languages (caribou reads it for the registry, the Haxe build macro and link-time thunks). Until now WrenLift only carried it as an opaque runtime attribute; caribou parsed it on its side.","message":"What WrenLift now does with it:\n- sema/export.rs owns the grammar (identical to caribou_wren::types: `name(a: T, _: T, b) -\u003e R`, `name -\u003e R`, `name=(v: T)`, function shapes `Fn(T) -\u003e R`), and the resolver reports a signature that does not fit its member (shape, arity, operators/subscripts) as a compile error on the attribute.\n- Declared `Num`, `Bool` and `String` are a contract: each such parameter is checked on entry and each return on the way out, through a new MIR instruction `CheckType { value, class, message }` (last variant of Instruction, so bundles keep their encoding). It raises like Fiber.abort (\"bump(_) expects Num for `x`\", \"bad returns Num\") in the interpreter, the Cranelift JIT and AOT, the LLVM tier and LLVM AOT (wasm). List, Fn and class types are not checked: caribou lets a Haxe array or function cross where they are declared.\n- The compiler relies on it: TypeSpecialize treats a Num check's result as a known Num (never its source, so it holds for returns too) and folds a check of a value already known Num; a declared Num result lets self-calls count as Num. MathGuard/inline-body rules: a declared body's specialised f64 arithmetic counts when deciding to inline it.\n\nFirst version (entry/return checks as IsType + branch + Fiber.abort call) made the JIT 4.8x slower on a hot declared method (0.12s -\u003e 0.57s for 20M calls): the extra blocks and the call stopped the callee from being inlined. As one instruction, lowered inline with a cold raise path, and with specialised arithmetic counting for inlining declared bodies: parity in the Cranelift JIT (0.120s vs 0.122s undeclared), LLVM tier 0.137s vs 0.155s, native AOT 1.32s vs 1.36s (AOT is dominated by dispatch and loop overhead: the same loop without the call takes 0.77s). Counting f64 arithmetic for every body, not just declared ones, cost delta_blue ~2%, so it is limited to declared bodies.\n\nFound along the way: AOT modules were lowered without any MIR passes (3d3513f).\n\nNot done here (needs the embedded Library entry, bed5dd5): C-ABI thunks named by caribou::link's symbol rule per exported member, and undefined references for Haxe members a Wren module calls. The thunks can call the typed body directly, which is where declared types pay off most in AOT.","files":null}]}